  "response": "I’m using the crimes codebase-risk skill to check the scan against the source and turn the findings into a scoped refactor plan.\n\n\nThe source shows several overstatements in the findings: `sveltePlugin` already returns `Plugin`, and `getRollupConfig` already has typed inputs and output. The useful changes are to narrow the internal contracts and expose side effects. Rollup’s `buildStart` is a required hook name, so renaming it would break cleanup.\n\n\nI ran `scan --format json` with the built crimes CLI v0.29.1 from the fixture root. It reproduced **82 findings**, including **eight findings across the three requested types** in these modules. [Scan JSON](/tmp/refactor-04-monorepo-scan.json)\n\nSource inspection shows several detector overstatements, so the plan should clarify real contracts rather than mechanically apply every suggestion.\n\n| Finding type | Reported locations | Severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `crime_00003`: `getRollupConfig`, `src/rollup.ts:38–159`; `crime_00008`: `buildStart`, lines 77–81 | Low |\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `crime_00014`: `build`, `src/index.ts:167–459`; `crime_00015`: `buildAll`, lines 291–365 | Medium / low |\n| `option_bag_junk_drawer` | `crime_00007`: `getRollupConfig`, `src/rollup.ts:38–159` | Low |\n| `return_shape_roulette` | `crime_00011–13`: `sveltePlugin`, `setup`, and the anonymous load callback, `src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9–124`, 17–122, and 31–121 | Low |\n\n1. **`name_behavior_mismatch`: make execution and cleanup explicit.**\n\n   In [rollup.ts](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/rollup.ts:38), `getRollupConfig` changes logging state and mutates `options.dts.entry` through an aliased object. Its `removeFiles` call is deferred inside a plugin hook; merely requesting configuration does **not** delete files immediately.\n\n   Split this into `prepareDtsBuild` for loading dependencies and configuring runtime state, plus `createDtsRollupConfig` for configuration assembly from resolved inputs. Copy declaration options before normalizing entries. Keep the required Rollup hook name `buildStart`, delegating its body to `cleanDeclarationOutputs`.\n\n   In [index.ts](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/index.ts:291), rename internal `buildAll` to `runBuildCycle` and extract `stopPreviousSuccessTask`, `cleanJavaScriptOutputs`, and `runSuccessHook`. Preserve the exported `build` API: building conventionally involves effects, so its name alone is weak evidence of a defect.\n\n   **What an agent could get wrong today:** reuse configuration preparation assuming it preserves its input; rename a framework hook and disable cleanup; or move cleanup across parallel JavaScript/declaration tasks and delete newly generated declarations. Preserve the existing declaration exclusion and execution order.\n\n2. **`option_bag_junk_drawer`: narrow responsibility and distinguish normalized inputs.**\n\n   `getRollupConfig` already accepts `NormalizedOptions` and returns `Promise<RollupConfig>`. The report’s “implicit shape” wording is inaccurate; the useful concern is that a declaration builder receives the entire build configuration.\n\n   Introduce narrowly scoped records such as `DtsBuildInputs`, `DtsOutputOptions`, and `CleanupPolicy`, derived from existing types. Resolve declaration entry defaults once, then pass a required entry into the configuration builder. Keep raw JSON compiler settings distinct from parsed `ts.CompilerOptions`.\n\n   In `normalizeOptions`, construct a complete object with an explicit `Promise<NormalizedOptions>` return type instead of relying on `Partial<NormalizedOptions>` followed by assertions. Copy cleanup patterns before adding exclusions: the current `unshift` can mutate the caller’s array.\n\n   Preserve the full context promised to `outExtension` callbacks at the adapter boundary. Separately define the serializable declaration-worker payload; currently it spreads broad options and manually removes functions.\n\n   **What an agent could get wrong today:** pass raw options where defaults are assumed, introduce a non-cloneable worker field, accidentally mutate reusable configuration, or narrow a callback’s input in a way that breaks consumers.\n\n3. **`return_shape_roulette`: type each callback’s actual protocol.**\n\n   [sveltePlugin](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9) already explicitly returns `Plugin`, and `setup` registers callbacks without returning their results. The detector combines nested return statements belonging to different contracts.\n\n   Extract named helpers with explicit boundaries:\n\n   - `resolveSvelteCss`: esbuild `OnResolveResult`.\n   - `loadSvelteComponent`: `Promise<OnLoadResult>`.\n   - `preprocessSvelteScript`: the installed Svelte preprocessing contract, preserving optional source maps.\n   - `toEsbuildDiagnostic`: esbuild `PartialMessage`, replacing `any` with typed diagnostics and narrowing caught `unknown` errors.\n\n   Keep valid esbuild success and error results. A discriminated union is useful only for an internal compilation result that needs narrowing; translate it into esbuild’s existing protocol at the callback boundary.\n\n   **What an agent could get wrong today:** return preprocessing `{ code, map }` from an esbuild loader that expects `contents`, assume an error result contains JavaScript, or access diagnostic positions that are absent.\n\nImplement these as small steps, first protecting cleanup ordering and input immutability, then narrowing options, then extracting Svelte callbacks. Verify missing-compiler errors, compilation diagnostics, TypeScript preprocessing, and CSS/no-CSS output alongside existing tests. Run the required verification and compare scan fingerprints afterward; disappearing findings alone do not establish correctness.\n\nNo source files were changed or tests run for this planning task.",
